@meta
id: 22-17
title: Change a column
chapter: 22
type: lesson
skills: visual-block
par: 26
minutes: 5
vscode: Alt+click on each let, Ctrl+Shift+Right, type const
open: order.spec.ts

@brief
`c` on a block deletes the rectangle and opens insert mode at its left
edge on the first line; on `Esc`, what you typed replaces the rectangle
on every line. The width of what you type does not have to match the
width of the block.

The seven stubs in the test setup are declared with `let` and never
reassigned; the linter wants `const`. Change the seven `let` to `const`.
Only those. The `deleteOrder` test further down contains the letters
`let` too, which is why a blind substitute is not the tool.

@keys
| `Ctrl-V` | start a block |
| `e` | in block mode, extend the block to the end of the word |
| `c` | change the block: delete it, insert on every line |

@hints
Put the cursor on the `l` of the first `let`. `Ctrl-V`, six `j` for
the seven lines, then `e` to make the block exactly the width of
`let`.
--
`c` deletes the seven `let`s and leaves you in insert on the first
line. Type `const`, `Esc`.
--
`/\<let` `Enter`, `Ctrl-V`, `6j`, `e`, `cconst`, `Esc`.

@solution
    /\<let<Enter> Ctrl-V 6 j e c const <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twenty-two keystrokes. `:s/let/const/` over the same lines would have
been fewer, and on this file it would also have been correct, because
the substitute is scoped to the selection and `deleteOrder` is outside
it. The block is the tool for when the column is what you can see and
the pattern is what you would have to think about. Both are right; the
block is the one you will not second-guess.

`e`, `w`, `f` and `$` all work to set a block's width, the same as they
set a character-wise selection's length.

@start order.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  let orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  let customerRepository = stub<CustomerRepository>();
  let paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  let emailService = stub<EmailService>();
  let logger = stub<Logger>();
  let clock = stub<Clock>();
  let provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService, emailService);

  afterEach(() => {
    jest.resetAllMocks();
  });

  it('deleteOrder removes the order and its lines', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).delete.mockResolvedValue(undefined);

    await provider.deleteOrder('ord_1042');

    expect(reveal(orderRepository).delete).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ where: { id: 'ord_1042' } });
  });
});

@expected order.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  const orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  const customerRepository = stub<CustomerRepository>();
  const paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  const emailService = stub<EmailService>();
  const logger = stub<Logger>();
  const clock = stub<Clock>();
  const provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService, emailService);

  afterEach(() => {
    jest.resetAllMocks();
  });

  it('deleteOrder removes the order and its lines', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).delete.mockResolvedValue(undefined);

    await provider.deleteOrder('ord_1042');

    expect(reveal(orderRepository).delete).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ where: { id: 'ord_1042' } });
  });
});
